WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.260
This chapter asks a specific question: Military AI can change sensing,

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:12.460
targeting, command timing, warning interpretation, autonomous engagement, and adversary expectations inside

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.715
already dangerous strategic systems.

4
00:00:15.055 --> 00:00:20.201
A model that is locally accurate can still increase catastrophe risk

5
00:00:20.201 --> 00:00:24.878
through compression of decision time, false confidence, interaction effects, proliferation,

6
00:00:24.878 --> 00:00:27.685
or loss of context-specific human judgment.

7
00:00:28.025 --> 00:00:33.318
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: A generic dangerous-capability or misuse chapter

8
00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:36.205
treats risk mainly as actor uplift.

9
00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:41.857
It does not own command authority, target and effect constraints,

10
00:00:41.857 --> 00:00:47.169
legal review, false-alarm propagation, adversary adaptation, crisis instability, nuclear entanglement,

11
00:00:47.169 --> 00:00:51.950
or the difference between component reliability and strategic stability.

12
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.881
The chapter's core claim is this: Military AI should be governed

13
00:00:57.881 --> 00:01:03.211
as a command-and-interaction system: deployment requires a declared mission and legal

14
00:01:03.211 --> 00:01:07.572
boundary, preserved accountable human authority, bounded sensing and action,

15
00:01:07.572 --> 00:01:10.480
adversarial and escalation analysis, fail-safe behavior,

16
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:16.863
auditable provenance, and prospective off-ramps; component benchmark gains alone establish neither

17
00:01:16.863 --> 00:01:22.357
lawful use nor strategic safety. Classify every use by mission,

18
00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:27.302
decision role, affected population, operating environment, command chain, reversibility,

19
00:01:27.302 --> 00:01:28.950
and escalation sensitivity.

20
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:34.793
Separate decision support, defensive automation, targeting support, and autonomous force application

21
00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:38.295
instead of assigning them one autonomy score.

22
00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:44.457
Bind authorities, target and effect constraints, confidence and abstention rules,

23
00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:49.115
communication-loss behavior, and human confirmation requirements before operation.

24
00:01:49.715 --> 00:01:53.745
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

25
00:01:54.085 --> 00:02:00.122
A non-operational governance prototype with a mission and authority register,

26
00:02:00.122 --> 00:02:06.160
legal-review record, decision-role taxonomy, sensor-to-recommendation provenance, abstention and safe-posture rules,

27
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.990
escalation-path scenario matrix, accountable approval log, incident schema,

28
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:17.041
and suspension/decommission procedure tested only on synthetic or publicly safe scenarios.

29
00:02:17.041 --> 00:02:22.751
Separate decision support, defensive automation, targeting support, and autonomous force application

30
00:02:22.751 --> 00:02:26.385
instead of assigning them one autonomy score.

31
00:02:26.725 --> 00:02:32.547
Bind authorities, target and effect constraints, confidence and abstention rules,

32
00:02:32.547 --> 00:02:37.205
communication-loss behavior, and human confirmation requirements before operation.

33
00:02:37.545 --> 00:02:43.995
Model interaction pathways including false alarms, compressed timelines, adversary spoofing,

34
00:02:43.995 --> 00:02:49.800
automation bias, proliferation, entanglement, reciprocal deployment, and crisis escalation.

35
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.243
The design can still fail. Automation bias converts an advisory system

36
00:02:56.243 --> 00:03:01.555
into de facto decision authority. Decision-time compression removes deliberation, verification,

37
00:03:01.555 --> 00:03:07.399
or diplomatic off-ramps. Spoofed, ambiguous, or correlated sensors produce confident false

38
00:03:07.399 --> 00:03:07.930
alarms.

39
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.975
Optimization for tactical success externalizes civilian harm, reciprocal escalation,

40
00:03:12.975 --> 00:03:18.725
or long-run instability. The correct response is to stop, narrow, quarantine,

41
00:03:18.725 --> 00:03:24.475
compensate, or retain an owned residual rather than narrate uncertainty away.

42
00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:30.121
This chapter remains Design rationale at argument support. Its contracts, examples,

43
00:03:30.121 --> 00:03:34.250
tests, or local artifacts establish only their encoded scope.

44
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:39.296
The chapter names proof targets rather than pretending they are already

45
00:03:39.296 --> 00:03:39.755
closed.

46
00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:45.299
A finite military-AI interaction record may hand off only when mission,

47
00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:49.556
authority, human judgment, effect bounds, escalation assumptions, safe posture,

48
00:03:49.556 --> 00:03:54.760
and residuals are explicit; no theorem authorizes a weapon or proves

49
00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.125
lawful use or strategic stability.

50
00:03:57.465 --> 00:04:02.889
Meaningful human judgment must be demonstrated for the actual decision context

51
00:04:02.889 --> 00:04:08.314
rather than asserted from interface presence. Abstention, communication loss, sensor conflict,

52
00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:13.245
and integrity failure resolve to a prospectively declared safe posture.

53
00:04:13.845 --> 00:04:18.809
No chapter claim or evidence state is promoted by this visual.

54
00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:23.773
A named mechanism is not proof of correct implementation or useful

55
00:04:23.773 --> 00:04:28.737
deployment. A local check or formal model does not establish real-world

56
00:04:28.737 --> 00:04:33.250
enforcement. No safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI, or ASI conclusion follows.

57
